module examples.check_capabilities

# plans/M7.md item A's accept case, 03-hardware.md §1 — the *positive*
# half of every rejection in the `err-cap-*` corpus, written so that each
# accepted line is one a sibling golden rejects when moved one struct over.
#
# What this pins:
#
#   - All four capability types resolve as ordinary type annotations, in
#     every position a driver needs: a field, an `init` parameter, a
#     private method's parameter, a free fn's parameter. The `--stage=check`
#     dump prints each resolved type, so a resolver that quietly dropped a
#     generic argument would move this file.
#   - A `@driver` **may** hold them (§1's own worked example holds
#     `Mmio[VirtioIrqMmio]`), where an `@actor` may not
#     (`golden/err-cap-actor-field`) — the asymmetry that makes the
#     containment rule a rule rather than a blanket ban.
#   - A `@driver`'s `init` is the one place a capability enters a program:
#     `golden/err-cap-actor-init` is the same signature on an `@actor`.
#   - A capability is a **resource** (§1: "unforgeable resource values"),
#     visible in the dump as `Struct RegsBundle resource` — a plain data
#     struct that holds one is a resource by composition, which is what
#     makes the containment walk see through a wrapper.
#   - Provenance accepts what it should, on both of its arms. `ack_vector`
#     is a free fn *taking* a capability; `RegsBundle.tag_of` touches one
#     only through its own body (its receiver is not a parameter, and
#     `self` is a capability-bearing value). Both are reachable from
#     `BlkDriver.service`, so both are reachable through the owning
#     driver's authority. `golden/err-cap-provenance` and
#     `golden/err-cap-provenance-body` are these same two declarations
#     with the driver's call removed — one line apiece.
#   - A `pub` driver method exporting only scalars is a safe actor API
#     (§1's "a driver may export safe actor APIs"), where the same method
#     returning or taking a capability is not
#     (`golden/err-cap-driver-export`, `golden/err-cap-driver-message`).
#
# **Why the capability field and the capability `init` live on two
# different drivers here**, rather than on §1's single worked example: a
# field must be fully initialized by any `init` the struct declares
# (02-language.md §3), and nothing in item A produces a capability
# *value* to assign — the mint is typed, not yet constructed. So a driver
# holding `Mmio[L]` can have no `init` yet. That is item A's honest floor,
# written into the fixture rather than hidden by omitting one of the two
# shapes; plans/M7.md items C and D are what give the field a value.

@layout(mmio, endian=little)
struct VirtioIrqMmio:
    @offset(0x060) interrupt_status: ReadOnly[u32]
    @offset(0x064) interrupt_ack: WriteOnly[u32]

# A second, disjoint partition of the same claim (plans/M7.md item C,
# 03-hardware.md §2: "two live layouts can never alias a register").
# `IrqPartition` below mints both — one directly, one through a wrapper
# struct — and is accepted precisely because their byte ranges do not
# meet. `golden/err-mmio-alias` is this same driver with the two layouts
# moved on top of each other.
@layout(mmio, endian=little)
struct VirtioConfigMmio:
    @offset(0x100) config_generation: ReadOnly[u32]

# The device type `DeviceCap[D]` names. 06-machine.md §6's closed device
# set ships with the stdlib and does not exist yet, so this stands in for
# it as an ordinary declared struct — exactly as `golden/appliance`'s own
# `BlockHw` stands in at `img.device[BlockHw](...)`.
struct VirtioBlock:
    id: u32

# A plain data struct holding a capability: legal (§1 restricts `@actor`,
# not every struct), a resource by composition, and the shape the
# containment walk must see through.
struct RegsBundle:
    regs: Mmio[VirtioConfigMmio]
    tag: u64

    # Provenance's body arm: no parameter here names a capability, but
    # `self` is a capability-bearing value, so this method holds hardware
    # authority and must be reachable through a driver — it is, via
    # `BlkDriver.service` below.
    fn tag_of(read self) -> u64:
        return self.tag

# 03 §1's own field shape: the driver "maps only what its ISR needs" —
# and 03 §2's claim partitioning, accepted: two live layouts, one held
# directly and one reached through a plain wrapper struct, consuming
# disjoint byte ranges (0x60..0x68 and 0x100..0x104) of this driver's one
# claim (plans/M7.md item C, `hardware.mmio.no-alias`).
@driver
pub struct IrqPartition:
    irq_regs: Mmio[VirtioIrqMmio]
    bundle: RegsBundle

@driver
pub struct BlkDriver:
    # Minted capability stored — this golden pins containment/provenance
    # typing, not claim (check-device-claim). Dropping `cap` is illegal
    # under 02-language.md §3.1 protocol consumption.
    held: DeviceCap[VirtioBlock]
    sectors: u64

    # §1's own worked-example constructor shape, minus the parts later
    # items own: `take pool: DmaPool[P, N]` needs item D to mint a pool
    # and `-> Result[unit, BootError]` needs item H's bring-up chain.
    init(mut self, take cap: DeviceCap[VirtioBlock], sectors: u64):
        self.held = take cap
        self.sectors = sectors

    # A private helper taking a capability: the driver delegating inside
    # its own authority. The same signature on a `pub` method is
    # `golden/err-cap-driver-message`. `read vector` is a loan — not an
    # owned drop obligation.
    fn service(read self, read vector: IrqCap[u32], read bundle: RegsBundle) -> u64:
        return ack_vector(vector) + bundle.tag_of() + self.sectors

    # A safe actor API: scalars in, scalars out.
    pub fn capacity(read self) -> u64:
        return self.sectors

# A free fn holding a capability, reachable only through the driver above.
fn ack_vector(read vector: IrqCap[u32]) -> u64:
    return 1
